//? Title: Influence functions for ridge regression
//? Lang: en

> Influence functions for ridge regression

A case-weight influence measures how the loss at a held-out point changes
when we slightly increase the weight of one training row. For weighted
ridge regression the gradient of the training objective is zero at the
fit. Differentiating that equation gives the coefficient derivative as
the negative Hessian inverse applied to the row's gradient. The derivative
of the held-out loss is its gradient dotted with that coefficient derivative.
One Hessian solve against the held-out gradient gives the direction needed
to compute every row's influence.

The example fits a line to eight points with a slope penalty of 0.3 and
an unpenalized intercept. We compare the analytic influence with central
differences from two separate refits per row. We also assign each row zero
weight and refit to measure the actual loss change on removal. Small
weight changes are what the derivative describes. The effect of removing a
row can differ from the linear prediction, which is the negative of its
local influence for a weight decrease from one to zero.

[Koh and Liang (2017)|https://proceedings.mlr.press/v70/koh17a.html]

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/influence_functions.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  constexpr int count = 8;
  const auto x = n::from_vector<double>({1,-3, 1,-2, 1,-1, 1,0, 1,1, 1,2, 1,3, 1,4}, {count, 2});
  const auto y = n::from_vector<double>({-2.8,-2.2,-0.8,0.2,1.1,2.1,3.2,1.0});
  const auto penalty = n::from_vector<double>({0.0, 0.3});
  const auto held_out = n::from_vector<double>({1, 3.5});
  const auto fit = [&](const std::vector<double>& weights) {
    return n::ridge_influence(x, y, n::from_vector(weights), penalty, held_out, 3.7);
  };
  std::vector<double> weights(count, 1.0), rows, numeric, removal;
  const auto full = fit(weights);
  const auto analytic = full.weight_derivatives.to_vector<double>();
  constexpr double step = 1e-4;
  double max_error = 0.0;
  std::print("| Row | Influence | Refit derivative | Removal loss change |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int row = 0; row < count; ++row) {
    weights[row] = 1.0 + step;
    const double up = fit(weights).loss;
    weights[row] = 1.0 - step;
    const double down = fit(weights).loss;
    weights[row] = 0.0;
    const double change = fit(weights).loss - full.loss;
    weights[row] = 1.0;
    const double derivative = (up - down) / (2.0 * step);
    rows.push_back(row);
    numeric.push_back(derivative);
    removal.push_back(change);
    max_error = std::max(max_error, std::abs(analytic[row] - derivative));
    std::print("| {} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} | {:.9f} |\n", row, analytic[row], derivative, change);
  }
  const auto harmful = std::max_element(analytic.begin(), analytic.end()) - analytic.begin();
  if (harmful != 7 || max_error > 1e-7) {
    throw std::runtime_error("Unexpected influence or refit discrepancy");
  }
  std::print("\nRow {} has the largest positive influence. Maximum refit derivative error {:.3e}.\n",
             harmful, max_error);
  n::save_npz("build/ridge_influence.npz", {{"design", x}, {"response", y},
      {"coefficients", full.coefficients}, {"analytic", full.weight_derivatives},
      {"numeric", n::from_vector(numeric)}, {"removal", n::from_vector(removal)}});
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.points(rows, analytic, "Analytic influence", {37,99,235})
      .points(rows, removal, "Actual removal loss change", {234,88,12})
      .lines({0,7}, {0,0}, "", {148,163,184})
      .title("Case-weight influence and full removal").x_label("Training row")
      .y_label("Held-out half squared loss derivative or change");
  plot.size(900,430).save_svg("build/influence_functions.svg");
}
```
